ogles2.library 1.3 for AmigaOS 3.x on PiStorm
=============================================

OpenGL ES 2.0 for 68k AmigaOS, drawn by the Raspberry Pi 4's GPU (Broadcom
V3D 4.2) through PiStorm and Emu68. The API is source-compatible with
AmigaOS 4's ogles2.library: the GLES2 functions plus the agl* context calls.

Requirements
------------
- An Amiga with a PiStorm and a Raspberry Pi 4B or CM4 (the GPU is the Pi's)
- Emu68 (tested: 1.1.0-beta.1)
- AmigaOS 3.x (tested: 3.2) with Picasso96 or CyberGraphX
- A 32-bit RTG screen for programs that render into a window

What is in this archive
-----------------------
  Install-ogles2      the installer (an AmigaDOS script, see below)
  Libs/ogles2.library the library
  Libs/v3dglslc       the shader compiler (Mesa's V3D compiler, 19 MB)
  C/OGLES2Log         writes the library's log while a program runs
  Examples/           two small programs with their compiled shaders
  SDK/                headers, import libraries, documentation and the
                      examples' sources, for programmers
  LICENSE, licences/  licences and notices

Installing
----------
From a Shell, in this drawer:

    Execute Install-ogles2

It asks before each step:
- copies ogles2.library to LIBS:;
- copies the shader compiler v3dglslc to LIBS: (optional, see "Shaders");
- makes the drawer SYS:OGLES2Cache and adds "Assign OGLES2Cache:
  SYS:OGLES2Cache" to S:User-Startup, so compiled shaders are kept across
  reboots (optional; without it they are kept in T: until the next reboot);
- copies OGLES2Log to C: (optional).
"Execute Install-ogles2 ALL" does all four without asking.

To remove it: delete LIBS:ogles2.library, LIBS:v3dglslc, C:OGLES2Log and
SYS:OGLES2Cache, and the lines between ";BEGIN ogles2" and ";END ogles2"
in S:User-Startup.

Using it without installing
---------------------------
A program finds ogles2.library and the compiler in its own drawer when they
are not installed: the library in LIBS:, else PROGDIR:libs/, else beside the
program; the compiler likewise (LIBS:v3dglslc, PROGDIR:libs/v3dglslc,
PROGDIR:v3dglslc). So a program can be shipped with a libs/ drawer holding
ogles2.library (and v3dglslc, if it needs to compile shaders). This works
for programs built with this SDK's import library (version 1.3 or newer).
A program's own copy also replaces an installed one that is too old for
it, unless another program is using the old one at that moment (AmigaOS
keeps one ogles2.library in memory for all programs).

To try the examples this way: copy Libs/ogles2.library into Examples/libs/
and run Examples/triangle or Examples/cube.

Shaders
-------
The GPU runs machine code compiled from each program's GLSL. ogles2 finds
a program's compiled form ("binary", a .v3dp file named by a hash of the
GLSL text) beside the program, or in OGLES2Cache: (or T:). When there is
none, the compiler v3dglslc compiles it (about 0.2 s per program; its first
start takes about a second) and the result goes into OGLES2Cache:, so the
next run needs no compiler.

Programs that ship their binaries (as the examples do) run without the
compiler. Programs that make shaders at run time (gl4es and the programs
built on it, for instance) need it.

The examples
------------
  Examples/triangle   a turning coloured triangle in a window
  Examples/cube       a textured cube with a depth buffer, in a window you
                      can resize
Close the window or press Esc to quit. Their .v3dp files are beside them:
they run without the compiler.

Logging
-------
The library logs to T:ogles2.log, or to the file named in the environment
variable OGLES2/LogFile (SetEnv OGLES2/LogFile RAM:ogles2.log). OGLES2Log
writes the buffered log while a program still runs. A program that fails
usually says why there.

For programmers
---------------
SDK/include    <proto/ogles2.h> (GLES2 and agl*), <libraries/ogles2.h>
               (tags, errors, extra enumerants), the dispatch table, and
               Khronos' GLES2/ and KHR/ headers
SDK/lib        libogles2.a (GCC) and ogles2.lib (vbcc), the import library
SDK/doc        ogles2.doc: the agl* calls, shaders and binaries, extensions
               and deviations from GLES2 and from AmigaOS 4's library
SDK/examples   the examples' sources and a Makefile (GCC and vbcc)

The import library and the headers are MIT-licensed: programs of any
licence may use them. The source of everything here, the compiler's
included, is at https://github.com/ladislawv/ogles2.

Conformance
-----------
The Khronos dEQP-GLES2 must-pass list (17,165 cases), run on an A1200:
all pass except 25 cube-map cases (the GPU always filters cube maps
seamlessly, as GLES 3 requires) and 6 timing and quality warnings. SDK/doc/
ogles2.doc lists the deviations.

Licence
-------
ogles2.library incorporates PiStorm3D's V3D backend and is distributed
under the Hyperion MiniGL Open Source License (licences/
License_MiniGL_Library.txt): for AmigaOS only, with the source published.
The import library, headers, examples and OGLES2Log are MIT. v3dglslc is
Mesa (MIT; licences/Mesa/). LICENSE lists every part.

